<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry I, Count of Champagne</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1127–1181</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Henry the Liberal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry I, called the Liberal, was count of Champagne from 1152 to 1181, son of Theobald II. He organised the Champagne fairs and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troyes">Troyes</a> the administrative and cultural capital of a powerful principality. With his wife Marie, daughter of Louis VII and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-aquitaine">Eleanor of Aquitaine</a>, he kept a court that welcomed poets such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chretien-de-troyes">Chrétien de Troyes</a>. He died soon after returning from a pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-i-count-of-champagne.jpg" alt="Henry I, Count of Champagne" width="320" height="345" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: G.Garitan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SCeau_Thibaud_07204.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
